# Guest-bound files MUST check out with LF line endings: they are shipped
# byte-for-byte into the Alpine guest (Copy-VMFile), where busybox sh /
# OpenRC reject CRLF ("\r" reaches the interpreter as part of the line).
# This repo runs on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which would otherwise
# rewrite these to CRLF on checkout — the same failure class the
# bundle.sha256 LF fix in launcher/guest_parser.py guards at runtime.
# (#655 Stage C; see docs/security/guest_parser_provisioning_record.md.)
scripts/guest/** text eol=lf

# Budgeted doctrine surfaces MUST check out with LF. Their byte budgets
# (tests/security/test_doctrine_freshness.py SIZE_BUDGETS) are measured on the
# working-tree file with stat().st_size, and were calibrated on LF content. On
# Windows with core.autocrlf=true these otherwise check out CRLF, adding one
# byte per line — enough to push CLAUDE.md (47,704 LF) over its 48,000 budget as
# 48,021 CRLF, failing the size gate on a clean checkout while the committed blob
# is fine. Pinning eol=lf removes the platform variable so the gate is
# deterministic (structural-absence over a hope-the-tree-is-LF configuration).
# Surfaced 2026-07-23 by the #1067 doctrine-figure merge; the handoff brief had
# flagged it as "worth its own ticket".
CLAUDE.md text eol=lf
docs/TEST_GOVERNANCE.md text eol=lf
docs/sprints/ACTIVE_SPRINT.md text eol=lf
.github/copilot-instructions.md text eol=lf
LESSONS.md text eol=lf

# Measurement subjects are evidence: the bytes a recorded probe consumed. On Windows
# with core.autocrlf=true these check out CRLF, so the worktree copy is NOT what the
# recorded run read — measured 2026-08-07, cook_ok.html was 23,713 bytes committed and
# 24,296 on disk. That file is the GROUND TRUTH of the 2026-07-22 gemma4 vision probe
# (cook_ok.png / cook_broken.png were rendered from it), so a re-render from the
# worktree would consume different bytes than the record cites. Marking every subjects/
# directory under docs/performance/ as -text removes the conversion rather than hoping
# it is benign; READMEs beside them are prose and stay normalised.
# (#1318, found by review; see docs/performance/subjects/README.md.)
docs/performance/**/subjects/** -text
docs/performance/**/subjects/*.md text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF. On Windows with core.autocrlf=true a .sh file
# checks out CRLF, and the CR rides into the shebang — bash reports "bad interpreter:
# no such file or directory" and the script is simply unusable, with an error naming
# the interpreter rather than the line endings. Same class as the measurement-subject
# rule above: a platform default silently rewriting content that is read byte-wise.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The git-native hook shim runs under git for Windows sh: CRLF would reach the interpreter.
scripts/githooks/** text eol=lf

# The containment evidence bundle is evidence of bytes: checkouts must be LF on every machine (its manifest and tests also
# hash LF-normalised bytes, so they do not depend on this, but the files must read the same everywhere).
docs/research/containment-evidence-2026-10-05/** text eol=lf
